# Floating point

## Separate stack

FP values live on the **FP stack**, not mixed with cells. Document `(F: … -- … )` in headers when a word uses FP.

Do not mix cell and float items without explicit conversion (`s>f`, `f>s`, etc.).

## Literals

Use exponent form — `1.` is a **double-cell integer**, not a float:

```forth
2e 2e f+ f.
1e    \ same as 1e0
```

Literals require **decimal** base (`hex 1e` is an integer).

## Stack manipulation

`fdup fswap fdrop fover frot` — mirror cell stack words on the FP stack.

## Arithmetic

`f+ f- f* f/ f** fnegate fsqrt fsin fln fmin` — prefix `f` on FP operations (see type prefixes in `forth-naming.mdc`).

## Comparison

Avoid exact `f=` — FP results are inexact. Gforth provides:

```forth
f~abs   ( r1 r2 -- flag )   \ absolute tolerance
f~rel   ( r1 r2 -- flag )   \ relative tolerance
```

Standard `f~` exists but is poorly designed; prefer `f~abs` / `f~rel`.

## Memory

`f, f@ f! floats float+ faligned` — for FP arrays. `sf@` / `df@` variants access IEEE single/double in external buffers.

## Example

```forth
: dot-product ( f-addr1 f-addr2 u -- r )
  0e swap 0 ?do
    dup f@ swap dup f@ f* f+  float+ swap float+
  loop  2drop ;
```
